theory.intro:
The **normal curve** is the graph of a probability density function, so an area under it is a probability. Its total area is $1$, it is symmetrical about the mean $\mu$, and its **points of inflection** sit at $\mu-\sigma$ and $\mu+\sigma$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.3cm, y=1cm]
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.2:3.2, samples=90, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[->] (-3.6,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (0,2.4);
\fill (-1,1.46) circle (2pt);
\fill (1,1.46) circle (2pt);
\draw (0,0) -- (0,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$\mu$};
\draw (-1,0) -- (-1,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$\mu-\sigma$};
\draw (1,0) -- (1,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$\mu+\sigma$};
\node[right, font=\small] at (1.2,1.95) {points of inflection};
\node[font=\small] at (0,2.8) {area $=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

