development.solution_text:
Both curves are symmetrical about $50$, so they have the same centre and the same peak position.
The standard deviation of $Y$ is twice that of $X$, so the curve of $Y$ is twice as spread out.
The area under each curve is still $1$, so the wider curve of $Y$ has the lower peak.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=0.9cm, y=1cm]
\draw[->] (-4.6,0) -- (4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-4.4:4.4, samples=100, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{3*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-4.4:4.4, samples=100, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{1.5*exp(-\x*\x/8)});
\draw (-2.6,2.2) node[left, font=\small] {$X$} -- (-1.1,1.64);
\draw (3.2,1.4) node[right, font=\small] {$Y$} -- (2.0,0.91);
\draw (0,0) -- (0,-0.12) node[below, font=\small] {$50$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

