mastery.question_text:
The curve of $X$ has points of inflection at $x=118$ and $x=142$. The curve of $Y$ has points of inflection at $x=124$ and $x=136$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.6cm, y=1cm]
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.4:3.4, samples=90, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.4*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[thick, dashed] plot[domain=-1.8:1.8, samples=90, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{3.4*exp(-2*\x*\x)});
\draw[->] (-3.8,0) -- (4.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\fill (-1,1.456) circle (2pt);
\fill (1,1.456) circle (2pt);
\fill (-0.5,2.062) circle (2pt);
\fill (0.5,2.062) circle (2pt);
\draw (-1,0) -- (-1,-0.14) node[below, font=\small] {$118$};
\draw (1,0) -- (1,-0.14) node[below, font=\small] {$142$};
\draw (-0.5,0) -- (-0.5,-0.72) node[below, font=\small] {$124$};
\draw (0.5,0) -- (0.5,-0.72) node[below, font=\small] {$136$};
\node[right, font=\small] at (1.5,1.5) {curve of $X$};
\node[right, font=\small] at (0.75,3.0) {curve of $Y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the mean and the standard deviation of each variable, and state which curve has the higher peak, giving a reason.
